pcm: move enable_oversampling out of the far end of the wave ROMs

pcm_t declares enable_oversampling after 15 MiB of wave ROM arrays, at offset
15,763,532.  PCM_Update() reads it once per emulated PCM sample, next to
config.oversampling at offset 2,112, so the flag costs a cache line of its own
that holds nothing else and is 15 MiB from every other field in the loop.
Moving it next to config is free and bit-exact: pcm_t is never serialised or
aggregate initialised.

No measurable effect on this host (the line stays resident either way); it is
one fewer line in the per-sample working set on a Pi 3, whose L2 is 512 KiB.

--- a/src/backend/pcm.h
+++ b/src/backend/pcm.h
@@ -77,6 +77,11 @@
 
     PCM_Config config{};
 
+    // Read once per emulated PCM sample, alongside config.oversampling. Left at
+    // the end of the struct it sits 15 MiB past every other field PCM_Update()
+    // touches, on a cache line of its own that holds nothing else.
+    bool enable_oversampling = true;
+
     uint16_t eram[0x4000]{};
 
     uint8_t waverom1[0x200000]{};
@@ -84,8 +89,6 @@
     uint8_t waverom3[0x100000]{};
     uint8_t waverom_card[0x200000]{};
     uint8_t waverom_exp[0x800000]{};
-
-    bool enable_oversampling = true;
 };
 
 void PCM_Write(pcm_t& pcm, uint32_t address, uint8_t data);
